A||||7||merlin||Do I need a new distributor?…||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||12-07-2001||06:39 PM||||I’m currently in the process of replacing the head gasket on my 1987 V6 GT and after removing the distributor, I noticed that as I rotate the distributor one of the prongs on the shaft is rubbing on one of contacts that is used by the pickup coil.  Does this mean I need a new distributor or that the shaft is bent, since it is dragging on only one of the contacts?  Can I just bend the contact out slightly or is this normal?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bazooka||12-07-2001||11:38 PM||||If I understand you correctly, it sounds like you feel the high points of the cam on the distributor shaft when you rotate it. Thats what activates the electronic signal thru the pickup coil and synchronizes the firing of the spark plugs with the position of the camshaft, valve, and piston. That sounds normal. Don't bend anything and don't fix it if its not broken, please. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||BC-GT||12-08-2001||12:28 AM||||Are you talking about these two points?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid28/p8f333670a099e38a29586bceeabdc411/fe1690ee.jpg.orig.jpg"> <BR>if so, I would try and bend it back and test it before splurging on a new distributor.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid27/pe8dae45bb8bf084e93628ae50c338631/fe1839db.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||merlin||12-08-2001||08:49 AM||||Yes, BC-GT that's the exact place where it's making contact.  Now, is it normal to have a slight drag on one or more of those contacts or should it rotate freely?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou||12-08-2001||09:12 AM||||Make sure that the distributor shaft does not have any side to side movement where the rotor goes. If it does your bushings are shot and you need to replace the bushing or buy a rebuilt ditributor as this is the number one cause of bent "tabs".||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||12-08-2001||02:40 PM||||As you rotate the distributors shaft and the contacts align themselves, you will feel a mild magnetic 'push' and it will feel like more torque is required to rotate the shaft further. <BR>This is a normal reaction, the mag pick-up coil uses this field for an ignition timing signal sent to the ECM. Makes your car run like it's supposed to sometimes. LOL<BR>Don't forget to install a new 'O'ring on the distributor.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||jelly2m8||12-08-2001||03:29 PM||||Contact Ed Parks at the Fiero factory, I remember him saying what that gap is susposed to be, only I can't remember if it was 0.025", or 0.040"<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||03:57 PM||||there should be no contact whatsoever, if there is any side to side movement, unfortunately, it is neccassary to replace the distributor<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
